  This has a number of simple and obvious answers to what to do next:  

  - Die:
    - The goal of survival doesn't matter at this point.  
    - So switch to maximizing the situation as a whole: 
      - Die and learn. 
      - Learning not to get into this situation in the future is the 
        best you can hope for.
  - 'X' for explore mode.
    - Since you're going to die anyway, this lets you maximize the    
      learning experience.
  - Savescum.
    - Since you're going to die anyway, this lets you maximize the    
      learning experience.
  - "Don't Do That Then"
    - "The best way to get out of trouble in NetHack is not to get into
      it in the first place."


  There are a number of lessons in proper preparation to learn here:
  - An early tourist should:
    - reserve the +2 darts for tough fast monsters like the leocrotta
    - save the expensive camera ditto
      - like instant self-invisibility
      - huge downside of making Elbereth useless on the leocrotta.
    - Not be on DL:6 at XL:4-, especially without pets.
  - Any player should:
    - have 1+ tough pets by the time a leocrotta is met
    - have improved AC with body armor, boots, helmets and 
      shields.  That's at least 5 AC, and probably much more.
  - Any player with telepathy and an uncursed blindfold should have 
    noticed the leocrotta much earlier and 
    - avoided it.
      - Blocking corridor with item on Elbereth
  - uncurse the cloak of magic resistance BEFORE you need more AC.
    - burnt -2 CoMR takes away two points of AC.  
    - Switching to +3 oilskin would improve AC by 6 points
  - Weapons:
    - skills are important
      - Trying to survive a leocrotta with an unskilled rusty short
        sword is non-optimal at best.
    - to-hit is important.
      - At unskilled low chance of hitting with short sword.
    - Need more ranged missile weapons for hitting leocrotta
      fleeing Elbereth.
      - Collect gems/glass for throwing in just such a situation.
        - Do as much damage as rocks and weigh 1/10 as much.
        - who cares if they're valuable?  Character survival is more 
          important.
      - Throw rocks if nothing else.
      - Even knives are worth throwing
        - +2 to hit when thrown
        - Might be enchanted, who knows
        - cursed missiles won't weld to your hand when thrown.
      - Darts from dart traps.

  Is the original situation possible as described for a careful player?
Of course.  The RNG hates everyone.  Is it worth agonizing over how to
get out of it (other than avoiding it in the future)?  No.


